WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.330
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:10.280
This paper is about creating stunning cloth
simulations that are rich in yarn-to-yarn

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.280
contact.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.610
Normally, this is a challenging problem because
finding and simulating all the possible contacts

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:22.590
between tens of thousands of interlinked pieces
of geometry is a prohibitively long process.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:28.150
Also, due to the many different kinds of possible
loop configurations, these contacts can take

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.310
an awful lot of different shapes, which all
need to be taken into consideration.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.660
Since we are so used to see these garments
moving about in real life, if someone writes

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:43.040
a simulator that is off just by a tiny bit,
we'll immediately spot the difference.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.309
I think it is now easy to see why this is
a highly challenging problem.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:53.079
This technique optimizes this process by only
computing some of the forces that emerge from

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:57.460
these yarns pulling each other, and only trying
to approximate the rest.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.129
The good news is that this approximation is
carried out with temporal coherence.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:07.259
This means that these contact models are retained
through time and are only rebuilt when it

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:08.439
is absolutely necessary.

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:13.990
The regions marked with red in these simulations
show the domains that are found to be undergoing

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:19.049
significant deformation, therefore we need
to focus most of our efforts in rebuilding

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:21.950
the simulation model for these regions.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.869
Look at these results, this is unbelievable.

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:27.389
There is so much detail in these simulations.

00:01:27.389 --> 00:01:30.240
And all this was done seven years ago.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.049
In research and technology, this is an eternity.

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:35.219
This just blows my mind.

00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:39.479
The results are also compared against the
expensive reference technique as well.

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:45.159
And you can see that the differences are minuscule,
but the new, improved technique offers a 4

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:47.469
to 5-time speedup over that.

00:01:47.469 --> 00:01:52.429
For my research project, I also run many of
these simulations myself, and many of these

00:01:52.429 --> 00:01:55.669
tasks take several all nighters to compute.

00:01:55.669 --> 00:02:01.649
If someone would say that each of my all-nighters
would now count as 5, I'd be absolutely delighted.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:06.319
